[THOUGHT] The patient denied weight changes but did not address other endocrine symptoms (menstrual cycle, libido, galactorrhea, fatigue, etc.) — so that question remains incompletely answered. However, the *absence of pulsatile tinnitus*, *progressive binocular vision loss*, *retinal hemorrhages/vascular findings*, and *lack of systemic red flags* collectively make cerebral venous sinus thrombosis (CVST) the leading diagnosis. The second most likely alternative remains a suprasellar mass (e.g., pituitary adenoma), but its classic endocrine manifestations have not been confirmed — and crucially, *a normal serum prolactin level would strongly argue against a prolactin-secreting adenoma* (the most common functional pituitary tumor), while an elevated prolactin could be *secondary* to stalk compression (“pseudohyperprolactinemia”) in any suprasellar lesion — including CVST-related mass effect or even severe intracranial hypertension. Thus, serum prolactin is a highly discriminating, readily available blood test:  
- Markedly elevated prolactin (>200 ng/mL) suggests a prolactinoma;  
- Moderately elevated (50–200 ng/mL) favors stalk compression (from mass *or* elevated ICP);  
- Normal prolactin makes prolactinoma very unlikely and supports non-secretory mass or CVST.  

Prolactin is more specific and accessible than full endocrine panel at this stage.

[TEST] Serum prolactin level

